598: \section{Logarithmic modifications}\label{sec-log-mod}
599: 
600: We now fill the family $X(u)\to \bP^1$ at $0$ and $1$ in a new way.
601: This logarithmic transform changes the fiber without
602: changing the torus family over the punctured neighborhoods of $0$ and $1$, see 
603: e.g.~\cite[Ch.~V, \S 13]{BHPV04}, \cite[\S 5.3]{EFGMS25}.
604: 
605: To this end, we first describe the family $X(u)$ over a neighborhood of $0$ and $1$.
606: Observe that the $\SL_2(\bZ)$ monodromies of $S\to \bP^1$ about $0$ and $1$ have orders
607: $3$ and $4$, respectively.
608: 
609: \begin{proposition}\label{reduction} 
610: Let $\Delta_0\ni 0$, $\Delta_1\ni 1$ be disks. Consider the order $3$, resp.~$4$
611:  base change $\Delta\to \Delta_0$, $s\mapsto s^3=t$ or $\Delta\to \Delta_1$, $s\mapsto s^4=t-1$.
612: \begin{enumerate}
613: \item For $i=0,1$, $S\times_{\Delta_i}\Delta$ admits a smooth elliptic reduction
614: $S'\to\Delta$, whose central fiber is isomorphic to $E_\triangle$ for $i=0$
615: and to $E_\square$ for $i=1$. \smallskip
616: \item The linearization, viewed as a biholomorphism $\phi\colon M^{\times,\circ}\to M^{\times, \circ}$,
617: lifts to a biholomorphism $\phi'$ of $M'\coloneqq \cO_{S'}(P'-O')$ where $P'$ and $O'$ are the transforms
618: of $P$ and $O$.
619: 
620: Furthermore, the action of $\phi'$ on $E_\triangle$ or $E_\square$ is trivial,
621: and $\phi'$ acts on the $\bG_m$-torsor $M'^\times$ (which over $E_\triangle$ or $E_\square$
622: corresponds to either a $3$-torsion or a $2$-torsion line bundle $L^\times$, 
623: respectively) by a scalar in $\bC^*$.
624: \end{enumerate}
625: \end{proposition}
626: 
627: \begin{proof}
628: The smooth reduction of $IV^*$ and $III$ Kodaira fibers after a monodromy-trivializing
629: base change is a well-known computation; see \cite[Ch.~V, \S 10]{BHPV04}. 
630: Let $S''$ be the resolution of indeterminacy of
631: $S\times_{\Delta_i} \Delta\dashrightarrow S'$. The biholomorphism $\phi$ pulls back 
632: to a biholomorphism $\phi''$ of the $\bG_m$-torsor $M''^{\times}$ over $S''$ corresponding to the
633: pullback of $M$. 
634: 
635: This lift descends to a linearization $\phi'$ of $M'^{\times}$ by Hartogs' extension---indeed,
636: the bundles $M''$ and the pullback of $M'$ agree away from the contracted curves of $S''\to S'$. 
637: So $\phi''$ descends to a big open set of $S'$ and then extends to a linearization of $M'$ over all of $S'$. The biholomorphism
638: $\phi'$ is equivariant
639: over the action of $t_{6P'}^{-1}$ on $S'$. By an explicit computation of $S''$ and the smooth model $S'$,
640: one checks that $P'$ meets a $3$-torsion point on $E_\triangle$ and 
641: a $2$-torsion point on $E_\square$. Hence $t_{6P'}$ acts trivially on either central fiber.
642: The corresponding restriction $L=M'\vert_{E_\triangle}$ or $M'\vert_{E_\square}$ is a
643: $3$-torsion or $2$-torsion element of ${\rm Pic}^0(E_\triangle)$ or
644: ${\rm Pic}^0(E_\square)$, respectively.
645: 
646: It follows that on the central fiber, $\phi'$ is an automorphism of the $\bG_m$-torsor
647: $L^\times$ (acting trivially on the base and by a scalar on the fiber).
648: \end{proof}
649: 
650: \begin{corollary}\label{fill} 
651: The quotient $L^\times/\bZ$ arising from Proposition \ref{reduction} is
652: an abelian surface
653: $(\wE_\triangle\times E'_0)/\mu_3$ or
654: $(\wE_\square\times E'_1)/\mu_2$. \end{corollary}
655: 
656: \begin{proof}
657: As $L$ is a $3$- or $2$-torsion bundle over $E_\triangle$ or
658: $E_\square$, its pullback to an \'etale $\mu_3$- or $\mu_2$-cover
659: $$\wE_\triangle\to E_\triangle\textrm { or }
660: \wE_\square\to E_\square$$ 
661: is trivial. Along this pullback, the $\bZ$-action lifts to
662: an action on $\bC^*$, whose quotient is an elliptic curve $E'_i$. The deck group of
663: the \'etale cover acts by translation on both the vertical and horizontal factors.
664: So the quotient is an abelian surface (rather than bielliptic).
665: \end{proof}
666: 
667: Abstractly, $\wE_\triangle\simeq E_\triangle$ and
668: $\wE_\square\simeq E_\square$.  We retain the tildes because the covering
669: maps are nontrivial isogenies of degrees $3$ and $2$, respectively.
670: 
671: \begin{corollary}\label{fill2}
672: After base changes of orders $3$ and $4$ locally near
673: $0$ and $1$, respectively, the restriction of $X(u)\to\bP^1$ to the
674: punctured disk admits a smooth proper extension
675: $X'\to\Delta$ by complex $2$-tori, with central fiber
676: as in Corollary \ref{fill}.
677: \end{corollary}
678: 
679: 
680: Let $X(u)_t$ be a smooth complex $2$-torus fiber over a base point
681: $t\in \bP^1\setminus \{0,1,\infty\}$, and put
682: $\Lambda\coloneqq H_1(X(u)_t, \bZ)$. Let $\gamma_0, \gamma_1,
683: \gamma_\infty\in \pi_1(\bP^1\setminus \{0,1,\infty\}, t)$ be standard
684: counterclockwise based meridians, ordered so that
685: $\gamma_0\gamma_1\gamma_\infty=1$.  Write the monodromy representation as
686: $$T_i\coloneqq {\rm Mon}(\gamma_i)\in \SL(\Lambda)\simeq \SL_4(\bZ).$$
687: 
688: 
689: Let us comment a bit more on the structure of $\Lambda$ and the rank $4$, weight $-1$ VHS
690: on the corresponding local system. By construction, the complex $2$-tori over a general point
691: of $\bP^1$ are $\bZ$-quotients of the semiabelian variety $M^\times_t$, which fits into the exact sequence
692: (\ref{exact}). Thus we have a natural filtration: \begin{align}\label{filt1} 0\to \Lambda^\times \to \Lambda \xrightarrow{\psi} \bZ\to 0 \end{align}
693: where $\Lambda^\times = \pi_1(M_t^\times)$ and a further exact sequence  \begin{align}\label{filt2} 0\to \bZ \to \Lambda^\times \to \Lambda'\to 0 \end{align}
694: where $\Lambda'$ is the rank $2$, weight $-1$ local system underlying the $\bZ$-VHS
695: of the elliptic fibration $S\to \bP^1$.  After good reduction at $0$ or
696: $1$, it specializes to $H_1(E_\triangle,\bZ)$ or
697: $H_1(E_\square,\bZ)$. 
698: 
699: 
700: \begin{proposition}\label{conj-rat} We have conjugacies
701: $$T_0\sim_\bQ \small{
702: \begin{pmatrix}
703: 0&-1&0&0\\
704: 1&-1&0&0\\
705: 0&0&1&0\\
706: 0&0&0&1
707: \end{pmatrix}},\, T_1\sim_\bQ
708: \small{\begin{pmatrix}
709: 0&1&0&0\\
710: -1&0&0&0\\
711: 0&0&1&0\\
712: 0&0&0&1
713: \end{pmatrix}},\,
714: T_\infty\sim_\bZ\small{ \begin{pmatrix}
715: 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\
716: 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\
717: 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\
718: 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 
719: \end{pmatrix}}.$$ 
720: \end{proposition}
721: 
722: \begin{proof}
723: After the cyclic base changes of orders $3$ and $4$, the family has good
724: reduction at $0$ and $1$.  Let $F_0$ and $F_1$ denote the resulting smooth
725: central fibers.  The isogenies in Corollary~\ref{fill} give
726: decompositions
727: \begin{align}\label{over}
728: \Lambda\simeq 
729: H_1(F_i,\bZ)\supset
730: H_1(\wE_{\triangle\textrm{ or }\square},\bZ)\oplus H_1(E'_i,\bZ).
731: \end{align}
732: Put $\rho=e^{2\pi i/3}$.  With the complex orientations, the counterclockwise
733: monodromy acts on the first summand by multiplication by $\rho$
734: in the basis $(1,\rho)$ at $0$ (type $IV^*$), and by multiplication by $-i$ in the
735: basis $(1,i)$ at $1$ (type $III$).  It acts trivially on the second summand.  Hence
736: \[
737: T_0\sim_{\bQ}\small{
738: \begin{pmatrix}
